  "_note": "术语表：这里的词在翻译前会被替换成哨兵，模型根本没机会改动它们。default 指向原文 = 「维持原文，不翻译」；给了某语言的覆盖值 = 「该语言有约定俗成的叫法」。",
  "_note_en": "Read the second half of the note above as advice about intent, not as a rule about completeness: a default that points at the source term means keep it as-is, and a per-locale value means that language has its own established wording. Adding a value identical to the default is normally the smell of an override that never took effect, so it is not done for whole-language convenience -- the per-locale entries added for th-TH are the exception that shows the boundary: a term whose Thai wording IS the Latin brand name is written out explicitly (so that 'this language has no override' and 'the wording is the brand name' are two different states in the file), while a term whose default is already Latin (bilibili / hololive / Tor / JSON / VTuber ...) needs no entry at all. The two readings of that boundary are conventions rather than truths, and vi-VN (the 29th locale) takes the Filipino one rather than the Thai one: an entry is written only where the Vietnamese wording is not the default, so a term whose Vietnamese wording IS the Latin brand name (bilibili / YouTube / Nijisanji / VSPO! / ServerChan / DingTalk / WeCom / Feishu / Moegirlpedia / VTuber) has no entry, and for vi-VN the two states around a Latin default -- this language owns no override, versus the wording is the brand name -- are deliberately the same state. The first half of that sentence has a case rule attached for vi-VN, because Vietnamese (unlike Thai) has letter case and the value here is substituted verbatim into the machine output: the wording is written as it appears inside a sentence, so a common-noun term is lower-case (thông tin / nguồn / theo dõi / giám sát / lưu trữ / hình ảnh) and the standalone labels that want a capital are written in the hand layer, which always wins. Only a term whose wording is itself a name keeps its capital (Ra mắt 3D, which appears as a calendar-kind label and inside keyword lists). Measured on the first machine pass before this rule: 74 rows carried a capitalised common noun mid-sentence, e.g. Dòng thẻ Thông tin and Đối tượng Theo dõi. The cost is that this file cannot tell those two apart for vi-VN; the gain is that every vi-VN entry here is a wording a reviewer has to check, and their count equals the count of terms the build insists on. The Thai reading is not wrong, it just trades that for the distinction: it writes the brand name out per locale. Switching vi-VN to it means adding those entries back, and this sentence is the one place that has to change.",
